Below is a minimal “DVD‑logo” demo that you can drop into an empty file called **dvd.html** and open in any modern browser.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="UTF-8">
<title>DVD Logo Demo</title>
<style>
  body,html{margin:0;height:100%;overflow:hidden;background:#000;}
  #logo{
    position:absolute;
    font-size:48px;
    color:#fff;
    user-select:none; /* prevent text selection while dragging */
  }
</style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="logo">DVD</div>

<script>
const logo = document.getElementById('logo');
let posX = 0, posY = 0;          // current position
let velX = 3, velY = 2;          // velocity (pixels per frame)
const fps = 60;
const step = () => {
  const w = window.innerWidth, h = window.innerHeight;

  // Update position
  posX += velX;
  posY += velY;

  // Bounce off walls
  if (posX + logo.clientWidth > w || posX < 0) {
    velX *= -1;                // reverse horizontal direction
    posX = Math.max(0, Math.min(posX, w - logo.clientWidth));
  }
  if (posY + logo.clientHeight > h || posY < 0) {
    velY *= -1;                // reverse vertical direction
    posY = Math.max(0, Math.min(posY, h - logo.clientHeight));
  }

  // Apply new position
  logo.style.left = posX + 'px';
  logo.style.top  = posY + 'px';

  requestAnimationFrame(step);
};

requestAnimationFrame(step);   // start animation loop
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

### How it works

1. **CSS** gives the page a black background and positions the `#logo` element absolutely.
2. The JavaScript keeps track of the logo’s position (`posX`, `posY`) and velocity (`velX`, `velY`).
3. On every animation frame, the position is updated, walls are checked, and if a wall is hit the corresponding velocity component is reversed (bouncing).
4. The new coordinates are written back to the element’s `left`/`top` styles.

Feel free to tweak the speed (`velX`, `velY`) or the text content of the logo.
